╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ optimize_ansatz_with_alternating_cnots                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ The original ansatz used a layer of RY gates, followed by a layer of RZ gates, a linear   │
│                          │ chain of CZ gates, and another layer of RZ gates. Because CZ gates are diagonal in the    │
│                          │ computational basis, they commute with RZ gates. This means the two independent RZ layers │
│                          │ separated by CZ gates were mathematically equivalent to a single RZ layer, introducing    │
│                          │ redundant parameters that flattened the loss landscape and worsened parameter efficiency. │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ To solve this and improve both parameter efficiency and trainability:                     │
│                          │ 1. We remove the redundant RZ layer, effectively reducing the parameter count per block   │
│                          │ from 24 to 16.                                                                            │
│                          │ 2. We replace the linear chain of CZ gates with two parallelized, alternating layers of   │
│                          │ CNOT gates (a "brick-layer" or "ring" topology). CNOTs don't commute with RZ gates,       │
│                          │ producing much richer interference and faster entanglement spread.                        │
│                          │ 3. The alternating CNOT layers drastically reduce circuit depth, improving trainability   │
│                          │ and keeping the optimization trajectory shorter and more stable.                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This improves overall test accuracy and parameter efficiency by structurally preventing   │
│                          │ over-parameterization while boosting the circuit's expressivity.                          │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.1636                                                                                   │
